by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-12T16:12:19Z

Sorry for the long wait on your question, and thanks for turning around every review point so quickly back in February.

The feature is wanted. What I did not want was `CollectionType` growing a second mode: `entry_types`, `entry_type_options`, `entry_type_prototype_options`, `entry_type_prototype_data` and `entry_type_provider` next to the five options it already has, all mutually exclusive with them. It is the type people already get wrong most often, and the prototype story is where most of that pain lives.

So I moved your work into a `PolymorphicCollectionType` and pushed it. `CollectionType` and its test are untouched now. Concretely:

 * Options keep the names they have on `CollectionType`, just keyed by entry name: `entry_options`, `prototype_options`, `prototype_data`. No `entry_type_` prefixes.
 * `entry_types` and `entry_type_provider` are required options rather than defaults validated later, so a misconfigured collection fails at resolution.
 * `EntryTypeProviderInterface` is unchanged, it is the part of your design I liked most. The two methods are what makes the model-data and submitted-data cases unambiguous, which was @stof's point.
 * `ResizeFormListener` is shared: `$type` accepts a map and takes an optional provider. That is the only change to existing code.
 * Block prefix is `polymorphic_collection`, with a `polymorphic_collection_widget` block rendering one `data-prototype-<name>` per entry type. Entries and prototypes still get `collection_entry` spliced in, so existing themes apply.
 * Your tests moved to `PolymorphicCollectionTypeTest` and all 27 pass. The 24 anonymous provider classes are now two fixtures, `NumericEntryTypeProvider` and `AuthorEntryTypeProvider`.
 * Rebased onto 8.2, CHANGELOG entry moved there too.

Form (5003 tests), TwigBridge (2002) and the Doctrine bridge (444) are green. I also checked that the tests fail when the provider dispatch is neutralised, so they are testing the feature and not the scaffolding.

Let me know if the naming works for you, `PolymorphicCollectionType` was the clearest I found.

